#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# n

#% text
# `n(e)` gives the numerical value of `e` as machine numbers.

#% text
# `n(e, d)` gives the value of `e` as balls with at least `d` correct decimal digits.

#% text
# `n(e, digits: d)` is the same as `n(e, d)`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# Machine numbers are double-precision reals with about 16 significant digits; `n(e)` evaluates in machine arithmetic.

#% item
# `n(e, d)` evaluates with arbitrary-precision balls: a midpoint and an error bound. The working precision starts a little above `d` digits and doubles until the ball has `d` correct digits.

#% item
# Only the correct digits of a ball are printed; often there are a few more than `d`.

#% item
# When `d` digits are not reached before a precision cap, for example because the exact value is 0, the result is `failure(precision_not_reached, best)` with the best ball found.

#% item
# `n` works through any structure: lists, equations and sums are kept, and every numeric part inside them is replaced by its value. Symbols stay as they are.

#% item
# A machine real anywhere in a numeric part makes that part a machine computation, even under `n(e, d)`.

#% item
# `d` must be a positive integer.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% text
# A machine approximation of `pi`:

#% cell
n(pi)
#% output
#= 3.141592653589793

#% text
# `pi` to 30 digits:

#% cell
n(pi, 30)
#% output
#= 3.1415926535897932384626433832795029

#% text
# The option form of the number of digits:

#% cell
n(sqrt(2), digits: 50)
#% output
#= 1.414213562373095048801688724209698078569671875376948073

#% text
# A fraction:

#% cell
n(1/3)
#% output
#= 0.3333333333333333

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# Each element of a list:

#% cell
n([1/2, 1/3, pi])
#% output
#= [0.5, 0.3333333333333333, 3.141592653589793]

#% text
# Complex values:

#% cell
n(exp(1 + im))
#% output
#= complex(1.4686939399158851, 2.2873552871788423)

#% cell
n(exp(1 + im), 20)
#% output
#= complex(1.468693939915885157138968, 2.287355287178842391208172)

#% text
# Special functions:

#% cell
n(gamma(1/3), 25)
#% output
#= 2.6789385347077476336556929410

#% text
# Symbols are kept and the numeric parts evaluated:

#% cell
n(x + 1/2)
#% output
#= 0.5 + x

#% text
# A large argument needs extra working precision, which `n` adds by itself:

#% cell
n(sin(10^30), 20)
#% output
#= -0.0901169019121380580303864

#% subsection
# Applications

#% text
# The exponential of `pi*sqrt(163)` is very close to an integer, which only enough digits reveal:

#% cell
n(e^(pi*sqrt(163)), 40)
#% output
#= 262537412640768743.999999999999250072597198

#% text
# A numerical value of a definite integral:

#% cell
n(integrate(sin(x), [x, 0, 1]))
#% output
#= 0.45969769413186023

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# A ball literal written with `~` is already an arbitrary-precision number:

#% cell
1.25~40
#% output
#= 1.250000000000000000000000000000000000000

#% text
# Two equal balls differ by a ball around 0 whose radius is the error bound:

#% cell
n(sqrt(2), 20) - n(sqrt(2), 20)
#% output
#= ball(0, 2.15e-25)

#% subsection
# Possible issues

#% text
# An expression whose exact value is 0 never gets correct relative digits:

#% cell
n(log(6) - log(2) - log(3), 10)
#% output
#! failure(precision_not_reached, ball(1.0116816456736594506e-963, 1.79e-963))

#% text
# A machine real in the input makes the whole computation machine precision:

#% cell
n(1e-30 + pi, 20)
#% output
#= 3.141592653589793

#% text
# The number of digits must be positive:

#% cell
n(pi, 0)
#% output
#! failure(invalid_argument, n(pi, 0))

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `chop`, `precision`, `accuracy`, `set_precision`, `rationalize`, `n_integrate`
